    {
      "id": "ES-C377-letra",
      "sequence": 7770,
      "title": "la letra — the word for writing, and nobody knows where it came from",
      "headword": "la letra",
      "romanization": "la letra",
      "gloss": "letter of the alphabet — Latin littera, which also gives English letter, literal and literature; where Latin got it is still unsettled",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8ed2521d55a18caf",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the chocolate, then the pepper. (El chocolate; El pimiento.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la palabra — what letters build."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la carta — the other thing English calls a letter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "LE-tra, two beats with the weight on the first. The tr runs together and the r is a single tap. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la letra — \"letter.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin littera was a letter of the alphabet, and English took it three times:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "letter, through French, worn down;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "literal, straight from Latin, meaning by the letter;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "literature, the whole of what letters have been used for."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Watch out for one thing in English. Letter covers two different objects: a mark on a page and something you post. Spanish keeps them apart. La letra is the mark. The thing you post is la carta, which you already have."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And here is the part that ought to be embarrassing and is only honest. Nobody knows where Latin got littera."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two attempts exist. One builds it on linō, \"to smear\" — writing as smearing — and it fails on the shape of the word rather than the sense. The other has Latin borrowing it from Greek diphthéra, a prepared hide people wrote on, by way of Etruscan. That one is still live and still unproven."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The word for writing is one of the words we cannot trace. Whatever else literacy did, it did not preserve its own name."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name a single character: una letra."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la letra",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la letra*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una letra",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una letra*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la primera letra",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la primera letra*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which Spanish word means a letter you post? (La carta — not la letra.) And does anyone know where Latin got littera? (No; both proposals are unproven.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C377-frase",
      "sequence": 7780,
      "title": "la frase — a way of speaking",
      "headword": "la frase",
      "romanization": "la frase",
      "gloss": "phrase, sentence — Greek phrasis, a way of speaking, through Latin; English phrase and paraphrase are the same word",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d4bf24ac25974615",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the pepper, then the letter. (El pimiento; La letra.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la palabra — the smaller unit."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "hablar — what you do with one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "FRA-se, two beats with the weight on the first. The fr runs together and the r is a single tap. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la frase — \"phrase.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek phrásis meant an expression, a manner of speaking. Behind it is the verb phrázein, \"to point out, to declare.\" The word came into Latin as phrasis and into Spanish from there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English has it twice, and the second one repays unpacking. A paraphrase is para- \"beside\" plus phrasis: a saying-alongside. When you paraphrase, you set a second version of the words next to the first. The word describes the action exactly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One useful difference from English. Spanish frase is wider than English phrase — it covers a whole sentence, not only a fragment of one. Una frase is something you can say and stop."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name a whole thing you say: una frase."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la frase",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la frase*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una frase",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una frase*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una frase en español",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una frase en español*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did Greek phrásis mean? (An expression, a way of speaking.) And what does para- add in paraphrase? (Beside — a saying-alongside.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C377-idioma",
      "sequence": 7790,
      "title": "el idioma — what belongs to you",
      "headword": "el idioma",
      "romanization": "el idioma",
      "gloss": "language — Greek idioma, a peculiarity of style, from idios, one's own; idiom, idiosyncrasy and idiot are all from the same idios",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:da46e062b4691cf4",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the letter, then the phrase. (La letra; La frase.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "español — the one you are learning."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la palabra — what one is made of."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "i-DIO-ma, three beats with the weight on the middle one. The io is one glide. The word ends in -a and is masculine — el idioma. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el idioma — \"language.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek ídios meant \"one's own, private, particular.\" From it Greek built idíōma, a peculiarity of style — the way of speaking that belongs to one person or one place. Late Latin took it, and Spanish took it from there."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three English words come off that same root, and the roads are worth walking separately:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "an idiom is a turn of phrase belonging to one language and refusing to move into another;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "an idiosyncrasy is a temperament belonging to one person;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "an idiot is, in Greek, an idiōtēs — a private person. Someone who held no public office and took no part in civic life. From there it slid to \"layman,\" then to \"non-expert,\" and only much later to the insult."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the word for a language and the word for a fool start in the same place: what is yours alone. One kept the pride in that and the other picked up the contempt."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A grammatical note you will need. El idioma ends in -a and is masculine. That -ma ending is Greek, and Spanish keeps those nouns masculine as a group."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name what you are learning: un idioma."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el idioma",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el idioma*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un idioma",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un idioma*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el idioma español",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el idioma español*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did Greek ídios mean? (One's own, private.) And what was an idiōtēs in Greek? (A private person, one with no public role.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C377-historia",
      "sequence": 7800,
      "title": "la historia — one Greek word that became two English ones",
      "headword": "la historia",
      "romanization": "la historia",
      "gloss": "story, history — Greek historia, knowledge got by inquiry; English history and story are the same word, story having come through Anglo-French",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a1ef273adf4fcbd0",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the phrase, then the language. (La frase; El idioma.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la carta — one place you might write one down."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la palabra — what it is built from."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "his-TO-ria, three beats with the weight on the middle one. The h is silent, so the word opens on the i. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la historia — \"story.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Greek historía did not mean the past. It meant inquiry — and then the knowledge you come away with after inquiring. A hístōr was someone who knows because they went and found out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The narrowing to \"past events\" happened later, and it is worth keeping in mind, because it turns history from a subject into a method."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English then took the word twice:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "history, borrowed formally from Latin historia;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "story, which came through Anglo-French estorie and lost its opening syllable on the way."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "For a long time English used them interchangeably. The split between a history that happened and a story that might not is only about five hundred years old."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish never made that split. Una historia is both — the account of what happened and the tale you tell a child — and which one you mean comes from the situation rather than the word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name what you tell: una historia."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la historia",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la historia*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una historia",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una historia*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una historia larga",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una historia larga*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did Greek historía originally mean? (Inquiry, and the knowledge you get from it.) And which English word is the same one worn down through French? (Story.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C377-baile",
      "sequence": 7810,
      "title": "el baile — two English balls, one of them unrelated",
      "headword": "el baile",
      "romanization": "el baile",
      "gloss": "dance — from bailar, borrowed from Occitan balar, from Late Latin ballare; English ball, ballet and ballad are the same word, and ball the round thing is not",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:288687e9008a2437",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the language, then the story. (El idioma; La historia.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la fiesta — where one happens."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la risa — what usually comes with it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "BAI-le, two beats with the weight on the first. The ai is one glide, close to English eye. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el baile — \"dance.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Late Latin ballāre meant to dance. What Spanish did with it is not the straightforward thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An inherited ballāre would have come out as ballar. Spanish says bailar, and Corominas reads that as a borrowing — from Occitan balar, taken up around 1270. The stray -i- has never been fully explained."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Go one step further back and the Academy's dictionary hedges twice. It offers Greek pállein, \"to shake,\" and marks the link perhaps both times. So the chain is: certain Spanish word, probable Occitan route, secure Late Latin verb, speculative Greek origin — four links of steadily decreasing confidence."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English inherited the middle of that chain and shows it off:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "a ball, the event you dress up for;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "a ballet, through Italian;"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "a ballad, which began as a song to dance to."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And then there is the other ball, the round one you throw. That is a Germanic word with no connection whatsoever. Two identical English words, two separate ancestries, and the one that means a dance is the one related to baile."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can now name what happens at a party: un baile."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el baile",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el baile*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un baile",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un baile*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "un baile largo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *un baile largo*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Did Spanish inherit bailar from Latin or borrow it? (Borrow it — from Occitan.) And which English ball is related to it? (The dance, not the round thing.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
